{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for gs batch at exact clean head 1797a4d31431a0cacfc04b503c4c849aa70182b7.\n\nMust fix: readBatch does not prove that the top-level JSON array consumes the whole input. At spike/cmd/gs/main.go:692 it calls decoder.More() after Decode; More is for checking whether the current array/object has another element, and a stray closing delimiter can therefore be mistaken for clean EOF. In an isolated initialized workroom I passed stdin containing a valid one-act array followed by an extra `]`. gs batch exited 0, reported landed=1, and appended event e934d4e4 instead of rejecting the malformed input before the sequence moved. This contradicts the request's whole-input preflight condition and docs/reference.md's statement that malformed input lands nothing.\n\nReplace the post-decode More check with a second Decode that must return io.EOF, and add a mutation-sensitive regression that supplies trailing malformed bytes/delimiters and proves head and depth do not change. The existing undefined-label test does not cover this parser boundary. Also add direct partial-prefix recovery coverage while touching the tests: the current retry test replays a fully landed two-act batch, but the requested recovery case is a retry after only a prefix landed.\n\nThe rest of the inspected design is sound: the command opens one workspace, reuses one resident submitter and actor key, leaves signing, payload ceiling, admission, dedup, sequencer signing, and CAS in the kernel per act, resolves prior labels to minted/replayed event IDs, reports landed/replayed/failed/skipped coherently, and preserves the existing HTTP submission path. Targeted TestBatch tests, make vet, make test, make race, make build, and git diff --check pass. Worktree remained clean; no merge or source change performed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:68766dbc8a1d4dab2fe5571ed6da10acdc4c0047","finding_count":"1","head":"1797a4d31431a0cacfc0